WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:02.360
(slurps coffee)

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:03.277
Gangster.

00:00:04.677 --> 00:00:05.606
(sniffs nose)

00:00:05.606 --> 00:00:08.105
(groans)

00:00:08.105 --> 00:00:10.895
That's the sound of a
good interview starting.

00:00:10.895 --> 00:00:13.441
(lively fiddle music)

00:00:13.441 --> 00:00:17.608
Trouble started with my
eyes when I was 16, 17

00:00:17.664 --> 00:00:20.206
and by the time I was 18,
I had to stop driving.

00:00:20.206 --> 00:00:23.470
Doctor said, "You legally
blind, you got 10/600 vision,

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:27.578
“you can't be behind no wheel and be safe."

00:00:27.578 --> 00:00:31.273
I have visual imparities,
but Legally Blind Boy Paxton

00:00:31.273 --> 00:00:34.170
is the stupidest stage
name I could come up with,

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:36.246
so I just omitted the 'legally'.

00:00:36.246 --> 00:00:38.820
(chuckles)

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:41.274
(mandolin picking music)

00:00:41.274 --> 00:00:42.721
- [Chris] I'm Chris Funk.

00:00:42.721 --> 00:00:45.450
I'm a musician in a band
called The Decemberists,

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:46.667
and I'm on a journey looking for the most

00:00:46.667 --> 00:00:50.349
surprising and extraordinary
people in music.

00:00:50.349 --> 00:00:53.751
My next stop takes me to New York City.

00:00:53.751 --> 00:00:56.003
There, I will meet a virtuoso in his 20's

00:00:56.003 --> 00:01:00.170
whose genius is playing
music that's 100 years old.

00:01:03.054 --> 00:01:04.192
You wanna play some piano?

00:01:04.192 --> 00:01:05.775
- [Jerron] Alright.

00:01:06.701 --> 00:01:09.681
(rolls keys on keyboard)

00:01:09.681 --> 00:01:11.189
(lively piano music)

00:01:11.189 --> 00:01:13.492
My name is Jerron Paxton.

00:01:13.492 --> 00:01:15.490
Born South Central Los Angeles,

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:19.656
currently of New York
City and a world traveler.

00:01:19.656 --> 00:01:21.585
I'm a music physician.

00:01:21.585 --> 00:01:24.252
Dr. Feelgood of music as it was.

00:01:25.357 --> 00:01:28.624
- [Chris] Jerron is a
master of forgotten music.

00:01:28.624 --> 00:01:29.684
The first time I heard him play,

00:01:29.684 --> 00:01:33.015
I thought I was listening
to some old, obscure 45's.

00:01:33.015 --> 00:01:34.702
He can pick up almost any instrument

00:01:34.702 --> 00:01:35.655
and it sounds like he just stepped off

00:01:35.655 --> 00:01:37.523
the Mississippi Delta.

00:01:37.523 --> 00:01:40.303
(lively piano music)

00:01:40.303 --> 00:01:41.965
(men chuckle)

00:01:41.965 --> 00:01:46.048
I play guitar, banjo,
harmonica, piano, fiddle.

00:01:47.695 --> 00:01:49.602
(fiddle music)

00:01:49.602 --> 00:01:50.756
And some other instruments that are

00:01:50.756 --> 00:01:52.894
reserved for private pleasure.

00:01:52.894 --> 00:01:54.297
(man chuckles)

00:01:54.297 --> 00:01:55.881
What?

00:01:55.881 --> 00:01:58.356
Get your mind out of the gutter.

00:01:58.356 --> 00:02:00.982
(fiddle music)

00:02:00.982 --> 00:02:03.421
People call this traditional music

00:02:03.421 --> 00:02:06.295
and lot of people tend to
think of me as a revivalist.

00:02:06.295 --> 00:02:09.172
I don't tend to think
of things in those terms

00:02:09.172 --> 00:02:11.026
because I'm a musician.

00:02:11.026 --> 00:02:12.784
This is music I always played and I always

00:02:12.784 --> 00:02:14.784
played it for my family.

00:02:15.897 --> 00:02:18.554
It's not old or traditional or throwback,

00:02:18.554 --> 00:02:20.538
I'm just a dude that plays music that

00:02:20.538 --> 00:02:23.675
my folks and I wanna listen to.

00:02:23.675 --> 00:02:26.665
Play the melody with the right side.

00:02:26.665 --> 00:02:27.498
No way.

00:02:27.498 --> 00:02:30.153
And you play the harmony
with the left side.

00:02:30.153 --> 00:02:32.823
I've got insane oral skills.

00:02:32.823 --> 00:02:35.240
(man laughs)

00:02:37.818 --> 00:02:39.315
- [Chris] One of my
favorite things about Jerron

00:02:39.315 --> 00:02:41.840
is that he's on a
historical mission of sorts,

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:43.596
rediscovering for the rest of us

00:02:43.596 --> 00:02:46.916
the original black music of America.

00:02:46.916 --> 00:02:48.492
People don't associate the banjo

00:02:48.492 --> 00:02:52.075
with black people
anymore and, you know, 200 years ago

00:02:53.002 --> 00:02:55.109
that's all you associated with 'em.

00:02:55.109 --> 00:02:56.172
(picks banjo)

00:02:56.172 --> 00:02:57.005
Oh, baby.

00:02:57.005 --> 00:02:57.878
(tunes down)

00:02:57.878 --> 00:03:00.338
That's my direction, to find a music

00:03:00.338 --> 00:03:03.336
that relates to my
culture I had to go back.

00:03:03.336 --> 00:03:05.694
I had to go back past recording history,

00:03:05.694 --> 00:03:07.429
had to go back to the 1840s to hear

00:03:07.429 --> 00:03:09.646
what that kind of music sounded like.

00:03:09.646 --> 00:03:11.928
And seeing as their
ain't no records of it,

00:03:11.928 --> 00:03:14.129
I have to recreate it, you know?

00:03:14.129 --> 00:03:17.569
I'm one of a handful
of black banjo players

00:03:17.569 --> 00:03:19.380
left in the world.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:21.657
I figured to represent black banjo music

00:03:21.657 --> 00:03:23.417
all I had to do was just act natural.

00:03:23.417 --> 00:03:25.238
♪ It may rain, it may snow, ♪

00:03:25.238 --> 00:03:26.842
♪ It may rain a whole lot more ♪

00:03:26.842 --> 00:03:29.373
Back home in South
Central, I'd play the music

00:03:29.373 --> 00:03:32.349
like my grandma taught
me from the cotton field.

00:03:32.349 --> 00:03:36.516
It don't have too much European
overtones and influences.

00:03:37.635 --> 00:03:40.038
The techniques and a lot of the melodies

00:03:40.038 --> 00:03:43.757
are made out of whole cloth
by the builders of America.

00:03:43.757 --> 00:03:45.593
(laughs)

00:03:45.593 --> 00:03:49.760
(crowd cheers and applauds)

00:03:49.806 --> 00:03:53.973
Thank you so much, my gangster
brothren and sistren.

00:03:55.902 --> 00:03:56.735
- [Chris] After watching Jerron

00:03:56.735 --> 00:03:58.407
at the Jalopy Theater in Brooklyn

00:03:58.407 --> 00:04:00.207
play songs that are 100 years old,

00:04:00.207 --> 00:04:02.872
it's easy to see why he
can captivate an audience.

00:04:02.872 --> 00:04:06.205
(guitar picks jovially)

00:04:12.899 --> 00:04:14.026
It takes me.

00:04:14.026 --> 00:04:17.167
You know, it's my music, it's my
culture and it's my passion.

00:04:17.167 --> 00:04:18.624
And I don't watch TV and I don't have

00:04:18.624 --> 00:04:21.713
too many other hobbies, so
this is what I get into.

00:04:21.713 --> 00:04:23.977
And I get into the history of it.

00:04:23.977 --> 00:04:27.068
(harmonica cries)

00:04:27.068 --> 00:04:28.580
It seems to be more entertaining than

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:30.711
any book and any story that you could read

00:04:30.711 --> 00:04:33.028
because it's all real
and there's more of it

00:04:33.028 --> 00:04:34.445
to be discovered.

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:37.987
(crowd cheers and applauds)

00:04:37.987 --> 00:04:38.997
Thank you, friends.

00:04:38.997 --> 00:04:41.580
(gentle music)

00:04:44.357 --> 00:04:47.190
(electronic beep)

